WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.672
Before you call your project an agent project, you should be able to answer a short design checklist.

00:00:06.672 --> 00:00:09.412
What is the concrete user goal?

00:00:09.412 --> 00:00:14.682
Why does this project need an agent rather than a fixed workflow?

00:00:14.682 --> 00:00:18.317
What unique capability does each tool provide?

00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:21.482
What information must stay in state?

00:00:21.482 --> 00:00:28.118
What can be refined if something fails: the query, evidence, draft, or plan?

00:00:28.569 --> 00:00:30.511
How does the workflow stop?

00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:36.101
What are the success criteria and hard limits?

00:00:36.101 --> 00:00:37.531
How will you evaluate it?

00:00:37.531 --> 00:00:44.181
Tool choice, retrieval quality, trajectory, and final answer should be considered separately.

00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:54.004
Which actions need approval? Anything that writes, deletes, sends, purchases, or approves should receive extra scrutiny.

00:00:54.638 --> 00:01:03.635
Before coding, try to write the goal, architecture, tool list, state fields, and stop conditions on one page.

00:01:03.635 --> 00:01:07.920
If that design is unclear, adding more agents will not fix it.
